===Etymology===
From early modern {{bor|de|nl|frikadel}}, which is borrowed from {{bor|de|fr|fricadel}}, an only later attested variant of {{m|fr|fricandeau}}. A further relation with {{cog|it|frittatella|}} is speculative. Compare contemporary {{cog|nl|frikandel}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˌfʁɪkaˈdɛlə/|/ˌfʁiːka-/}}
* {{audio|de|De-Frikadelle.ogg|Audio}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|f||Frikadellen}}

# {{lb|de|regional}} [[meatball]]

====Usage notes====
* ''Frikadelle'' is the most widely accepted word for “meatball” in German. It is nevertheless regionally bound and heard most often in central-western and north-western Germany.
* The typically German way of preparing meatballs see at {{m|en|frikadelle}}. The word is not restricted to this particular recipe, however.

